Lab Tasks
Task 1: View File Permissions with ls -l
Objective: Learn how to interpret file permissions using the ls -l command.

Step 1: List Files with Detailed Permissions
Run the following command to view file permissions in the current directory:

ls -l
Expected Output:

-rw-r--r-- 1 user group 1024 Jun 10 10:00 example.txt
drwxr-xr-x 2 user group 4096 Jun 10 10:01 directory
Step 2: Interpret Permission Strings
File Type:
- (Regular file)
d (Directory)
Permissions:
rw- (Owner: Read & Write)
r-- (Group: Read-only)
r-- (Others: Read-only)
Key Concept:

u (User/Owner), g (Group), o (Others)
r (Read), w (Write), x (Execute)
Task 2: Change File Permissions Using chmod
Objective: Modify file permissions using symbolic and numeric modes.

Step 1: Change Permissions Symbolically
Grant execute permission to the owner of example.txt:

chmod u+x example.txt
Verify changes:

ls -l example.txt
Expected Output:

-rwxr--r-- 1 user group 1024 Jun 10 10:00 example.txt
Step 2: Change Permissions Numerically
Set permissions to rw-r----- (640) for example.txt:

chmod 640 example.txt
Verify changes:

ls -l example.txt
Expected Output:

-rw-r----- 1 user group 1024 Jun 10 10:00 example.txt
Key Concept:

4 (Read), 2 (Write), 1 (Execute)
6 (4+2 = Read+Write), 4 (4+0 = Read-only)
Troubleshooting Tip:
If permission denied, ensure you have sufficient privileges (sudo may be required).

Task 3: Modify File Ownership Using chown and chgrp
Objective: Change file owner and group ownership.

Step 1: Change File Owner
Change the owner of example.txt to a different user (newuser):

sudo chown newuser example.txt
Verify changes:

ls -l example.txt
Expected Output:

-rw-r----- 1 newuser group 1024 Jun 10 10:00 example.txt
Step 2: Change File Group
Change the group of example.txt to newgroup:

sudo chgrp newgroup example.txt
Verify changes:

ls -l example.txt
Expected Output:

-rw-r----- 1 newuser newgroup 1024 Jun 10 10:00 example.txt
Key Concept:

chown modifies both owner and group (chown user:group file)
